## What to run now

**1. In Cloud Shell: per-minute CPU of the backend servers.** It shows whether CPU crossed 60% before `p9rn` appeared, and whether it's still high:
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
curl -s -G -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)" "https://monitoring.googleapis.com/v3/projects/$P/timeSeries" \
 --data-urlencode 'filter=metric.type="compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/utilization" AND metric.labels.instance_name=starts_with("cn-backend-mig-regional")' \
 --data-urlencode "interval.startTime=2026-10-05T09:38:00Z" --data-urlencode "interval.endTime=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" \
 --data-urlencode "aggregation.alignmentPeriod=60s" --data-urlencode "aggregation.perSeriesAligner=ALIGN_MEAN" \
 | jq -r '.timeSeries[]? | .metric.labels.instance_name as $n | .points[] | "\($n) \(.interval.endTime) \(.value.doubleValue)"' | sort -k2 | awk '{printf "%s %s %.0f%%\n",$1,substr($2,12,5),$3*100}'
```
**2. On the backend server: current CPU rather than a lifetime average.** This takes two samples 5 seconds apart and keeps the second:
```bash
