```

## Reading the CPU output

| What you see | Meaning | Likely fix |
|---|---|---|
| a CPU spike in the first minutes after each server's creation, then idle | boot-time CPU | raise the autoscaler cool-down so it ignores a new server's first 5 minutes. Console: Instance groups → `cn-backend-mig-regional` → Edit → Autoscaling → Cool-down `300`. No cost. Rollback: set it back to 60 |
| a spike 25 to 60 minutes after boot | the upgrade timer again | check whether the upgrade log shows packages. If it does, the image patching didn't fully work |
| steady CPU above 60% | real load, or something running continuously | I'd look at what's running before changing anything |
| CPU low everywhere but the target is 2 | the recommendation comes from somewhere I haven't seen | I'd ask for the autoscaler's full configuration |

I withdrew the cool-down idea earlier because those spikes arrived 25 to 62 minutes after boot, and it fits only if this data shows a boot-time spike.

## Recheck the upgrade timing around 10:45 UTC

That's 60 minutes after `khkg` booted. From the backend server:
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
for S in backend frontend; do
  N=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-$S-mig" --format="value(name)" --limit=1)
  Z=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-$S-mig" --format="value(zone.basename())" --limit=1)
  echo "== $N"
  gcloud compute ssh $N --zone=$Z --project=$P --internal-ip --command="uptime; sudo tail -n 4 /var/log/unattended-upgrades/unattended-upgrades.log | cut -c1-170; systemctl list-timers --all --no-pager | grep apt"
done
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list --project=$P --format="table(name,size,targetSize)"
```
Success: the log shows no packages or only a few, and the frontend stays at 1. The backend extra server may remain until the autoscaler's 600-second scale-in wait ends.

